+
+    THE SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED "AS IS", WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND, EXPRESS OR
+    IMPLIED, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO THE WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY,
+    FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE AND NONINFRINGEMENT.  IN NO EVENT SHALL
+    THE AUTHORS OR COPYRIGHT HOLDERS BE LIABLE FOR ANY CLAIM, DAMAGES OR OTHER
+    LIABILITY, WHETHER IN AN ACTION OF CONTRACT, TORT OR OTHERWISE, ARISING
+    FROM, OUT OF OR IN CONNECTION WITH THE SOFTWARE OR THE USE OR OTHER
+    DEALINGS IN THE SOFTWARE.
+  </copyright>
+
+  <description summary="system-wide back navigation for toplevels">
+    This protocol lets a client tell the compositor whether a "back" action
+    currently has an effect in one of its toplevels, and lets the compositor
+    ask the client to go back in that toplevel.
+
+    It is meant for environments where the system offers one back control for
+    all applications, for example a back button in the navigation bar of a
+    phone shell, a back gesture or a hardware back key. Such a control replaces
+    the per-application back buttons the user would otherwise have to reach in
+    the window content. The compositor decides how and when the control is
+    presented; this protocol only carries the state of the toplevel and the
+    back request.
+
+    A toplevel without a kde_back_navigation_v1 object, and every toplevel of a
+    compositor that does not offer this protocol, behaves as without it: back
+    is never available for it and no back event is ever sent to it.
+
+    Warning! The protocol described in this file is currently in the testing
+    phase. Backward compatible changes may be added together with the
+    corresponding interface version bump. Backward incompatible changes can
+    only be done by creating a new major version of the extension.
+  </description>
+
+  <interface name="kde_back_navigation_manager_v1" version="1">
+    <description summary="create back navigation objects for toplevels">
+      A global that creates kde_back_navigation_v1 objects for xdg_toplevels.
+    </description>
+
+    <enum name="error">
+      <entry name="already_constructed" value="0"
+        summary="the xdg_toplevel already has a kde_back_navigation_v1 object"/>
+    </enum>
+
+    <request name="destroy" type="destructor">
+      <description summary="destroy the manager">
+        Destroy the manager. Objects created through it are not affected.
+      </description>
+    </request>
+
+    <request name="get_back_navigation">
+      <description summary="create a back navigation object for a toplevel">
+        Create a kde_back_navigation_v1 object for the given xdg_toplevel. Its
+        state is initially unavailable.
+
+        The object may be created at any time in the life of the toplevel,
+        before or after it is mapped. Creating a second object for a toplevel
+        that already has one is a protocol error (already_constructed); after
+        the first object is destroyed a new one may be created.
+      </description>
+      <arg name="id" type="new_id" interface="kde_back_navigation_v1"/>
+      <arg name="toplevel" type="object" interface="xdg_toplevel"/>
+    </request>
+  </interface>
+
+  <interface name="kde_back_navigation_v1" version="1">
+    <description summary="back navigation state and requests of a toplevel">
+      Carries the back navigation state of one xdg_toplevel and the back
+      requests the compositor sends to it.
+
+      The state and the back event refer to this toplevel only. Every
+      xdg_toplevel has its own state, also one with a parent
+      (xdg_toplevel.set_parent) or one that is a dialog (xdg_dialog_v1). The
+      compositor never sends back to a toplevel because of the state of
+      another one, and never forwards a back action of a dialog to its parent
+      or of a parent to its dialog. A dialog that leaves its state unavailable
+      is handled as without this protocol: the compositor may offer to close
+      it (xdg_toplevel.close) instead.
+
+      xdg_popups have no state of their own. A compositor that dismisses open
+      popups when the user operates its back control should not also send
+      back for that interaction.
+
+      The object stays valid while the toplevel is unmapped and mapped again,
+      and keeps its state. If the xdg_toplevel is destroyed first, the object
+      becomes inert: requests have no effect and no events are sent. Destroying
+      this object makes the state of the toplevel unavailable.
+    </description>
+
+    <enum name="state">
+      <entry name="unavailable" value="0"
+        summary="back has no effect in the toplevel"/>
+      <entry name="available" value="1"
+        summary="back goes back one step in the toplevel"/>
+    </enum>
+
+    <enum name="error">
+      <entry name="invalid_state" value="0"
+        summary="the state is not a value of the state enum"/>
+    </enum>
+
+    <request name="destroy" type="destructor">
+      <description summary="destroy the back navigation object">
+        Destroy the object. The state of the toplevel becomes unavailable
+        immediately.
+      </description>
+    </request>
+
+    <request name="set_state">
+      <description summary="set whether back has an effect">
+        Set whether a back event received now would have an effect in the
+        toplevel. The new state applies as soon as the compositor processes
+        this request; it is not double-buffered and needs no wl_surface.commit,
+        because it does not describe the content of the surface.
+
+        The state must be available exactly when a back event would go back
+        one step within the toplevel, as the back button or the Back key of the
+        application itself would: return to the previous page or view, close an
+        overlay drawn inside the toplevel (a drawer, a sheet, an in-window
+        dialog, a search field), or leave a mode such as a selection or an
+        editing mode. It must be unavailable when back would do nothing, and
+        when the only thing it could do is to close the toplevel or to quit the
+        application: closing is offered by the compositor by other means.
+
+        The client keeps the state up to date at all times, also while the
+        toplevel is not focused, minimized or unmapped, so that the compositor
+        can present the right control as soon as the toplevel becomes active.
+        Setting the state it already has is allowed and has no effect.
+
+        A value that is not part of the state enum is a protocol error
+        (invalid_state).
+      </description>
+      <arg name="state" type="uint" enum="state"/>
+    </request>
+
+    <event name="back">
+      <description summary="go back one step">
+        The user asked to go back in this toplevel. The client goes back one
+        step, as described for set_state, exactly as if its own back control
+        had been used, and updates the state afterwards.
+
+        The compositor sends this event only in response to a user action and
+        only while the state is available as far as it knows. Because the
+        client can change the state at any time, the event may arrive after the
+        client has made the state unavailable. A client that has nothing to go
+        back to when it handles the event must ignore it; in particular it must
+        not close the toplevel or quit the application because of it.
+
+        The compositor does not wait for an answer and sends no further event
+        for the same action. A compositor should not send this event to a
+        toplevel that currently cannot receive user input, for example while it
+        is blocked by a modal dialog or while the session is locked.
+      </description>
+    </event>
+  </interface>
+</protocol>
